 "answer_text": "Found 3 gene records for Li-Fraumeni syndrome: tumor protein p53 [1], checkpoint kinase 2 [2] and cyclin dependent kinase inhibitor 2A [3], linked to 1 disease: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [5]. \n\nLi-Fraumeni syndrome is associated with three genes in the retrieved graph records: tumor protein p53 (NCBIGene:7157) [1], checkpoint kinase 2 (NCBIGene:11200) [2], and cyclin dependent kinase inhibitor 2A (NCBIGene:1029) [3]. \n\nThe syndrome follows autosomal dominant inheritance with high penetrance and confers a very high lifetime cumulative risk of multiple malignancies [4]. The component tumors include soft tissue sarcomas, osteosarcoma, premenopausal breast cancer, brain tumors, and adrenal cortical carcinomas [4]. \n\nDisease records found\n\nDisease name: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [5]. \n\nGene-to-disease mapping\n\nGene NCBIGene:1029, name: cyclin dependent kinase inhibitor 2A [3]. Gene NCBIGene:11200, name: checkpoint kinase 2 [2]. Gene NCBIGene:7157, name: tumor protein p53 [1]. \n\nPubmed records found\n\nPubmed title: Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [6]. Pubmed title: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [11]. Pubmed title: Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [13]. Pubmed title: Li-Fraumeni syndrome heterogeneity [15]. \n\nMedgen records found\n\nMedgen title: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [7]. Medgen clinical_features: MedGen lists no clinical features for Li-Fraumeni syndrome [8]. \n\nLiterature entity records found\n\nLiterature entity name: Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [9]. \n\nClinical trial records found\n\nClinical trial name: Clinical, Genetic, Behavioral, Laboratory and Epidemiologic Characterization of Individuals and Families at High Risk of Breast/Ovarian Cancer [10]. Clinical trial name: Role of p53 Gene in Metabolism Regulation in Patients With Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [12]. Clinical trial name: Characterization of Patients With Uncommon Presentations and/or Uncommon Diseases Associated With the Cardiovascular System [14]. Clinical trial name: Clinical and Genetic Studies of Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [16]. Clinical trial name: LIFSCREEN : Evaluation of Whole Body MRI for Early Detection of Cancers in Subjects With P53 Mutation (Li-Fraumeni Syndrome) [17]. "
